<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Painful, stiff hands in an 85-year-old working sculptor, worst at the left thumb base, with metacarpophalangeal as well as interphalangeal involvement. Please confirm osteoarthritis, exclude inflammatory, crystal, and iron-overload arthritis, and advise on a thumb-base injection and on safe pain relief that lets him keep carving. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree this is osteoarthritis, and is anything else, such as calcium pyrophosphate disease, hemochromatosis, gout, or an inflammatory arthritis, contributing given the metacarpophalangeal involvement?</item><item>Is a glucocorticoid injection of the left thumb base appropriate, and would you perform it?</item><item>What pain relief is safe at 85 with gout, a past stone history, and a creatinine rise on ibuprofen?</item><item>What splinting, hand therapy, and tool changes do you advise so he can keep carving?</item><item>Does his gout need any change in treatment because of the hand symptoms?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Buonarroti is seen at the request of Dr. Castellano for hand pain. He has carved stone all his working life and still works most mornings. Over about two years the base of his left thumb has become painful and stiff, with knobby swelling of the thumb and index finger and, more recently, the right index and middle fingertips. Morning stiffness lasts about 15 minutes. He has had no hot, swollen hand joints, no rash, psoriasis, or nail change, and no other joint symptoms apart from his past gout, which has been quiet on allopurinol. After four weeks off ibuprofen and on diclofenac gel and a splint, his pain is about 5 of 10. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Michelangelo was born at Caprese on March 6, 1475, by the modern reckoning; his father&apos;s notebook records the birth as March 6, 1474, in the Florentine style, on a Monday morning before daybreak. [D]</item><item>In 1548 he wrote to his nephew that he had lately been in great difficulty from dysuria but was better. [D]</item><item>In a letter to his nephew dated March 15, 1549, he wrote that he had been very ill with dysuria, groaning in pain night and day without sleep, that his doctors thought he was suffering from calculus, and that he had been advised to try the baths at Viterbo. [D]</item><item>On March 23, 1549, he wrote that after drinking a water sent to him he passed a large quantity of thick white matter with portions of the shell of the stone, and was much better. [D]</item><item>On April 5, 1549, he wrote that it was now quite certain that he had urinary calculus, not very advanced, and that it was gradually diminishing with the water he was drinking. [D]</item><item>In a letter the editor dates to June 8, 1549, he wrote that for about two months he had drunk, morning and evening, a water from a spring about forty miles from Rome that breaks up the stone, that it had broken his and let him pass water with tolerable ease, and that he would have to use nothing else for drinking or cooking and alter his mode of life. [D]</item><item>On June 16, 1557, he wrote to his nephew that he suffered from all the ailments of old age: the stone, so that he could not pass water, and pains in his side and back, so that he often could not go up or down stairs. [D]</item><item>A postscript to a letter of 1559 (dated June 15; the editor notes the nephew altered it to July 15) reads: writing is very troublesome to my hand, my eyes and my memory; such is the effect of age. [D]</item><item>Lazzeri and colleagues (2016), studying portraits, proposed that degenerative, non-inflammatory osteoarthritis affected the base of his left thumb, the thumb&apos;s metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints, and the index finger&apos;s metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints; earlier portraits show no deformity, and they place the onset around 1547 to 1553, with worsening in his last fifteen years. [R]</item><item>The same authors report that letters to his nephew speak of gout, a period term for many arthritic conditions, and of a cruel pain in one foot; they judged gouty arthritis of the hands unlikely because the portraits show no inflammation or tophi. [R]</item><item>His father, Lodovico, was born on June 11, 1444, and died in 1533 or 1534; Michelangelo&apos;s poem on his death says he was ninety. [D]</item><item>He had two acute illnesses, the first in the summer of 1544 and the second toward the close of 1545, when news reached Florence that he was dying; he recovered from both. [D]</item></list><paragraph>3 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

