    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Primary osteoarthritis of the left hand, nodal and non-erosive, worst at the thumb base (M19.042). Confirmed. The bony swelling without synovitis, normal inflammatory markers, negative rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP, and the radiographs fit osteoarthritis. Metacarpophalangeal disease here follows a lifetime of heavy hand use; there is no chondrocalcinosis or hook-like osteophyte to suggest calcium pyrophosphate disease, and normal iron studies make hemochromatosis arthropathy unlikely. [S]</item><item>Mild nodal osteoarthritis of the right hand (M19.041). Typical Heberden nodes; little pain. [S]</item><item>Gout in remission (M1A.9XX0). Not contributing: no tophi, no warmth, and urate at target. No change to allopurinol is needed. [S]</item><item>Analgesic safety. Regular oral NSAIDs are best avoided at his age because of the kidney and blood pressure changes already seen; creatinine is back to 1.02 mg/dL off ibuprofen. Topical diclofenac, acetaminophen up to 3 g a day, and injections are the safer options. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Intake radiographs of both hands; the intake blood count, kidney function, urate, CRP, ESR, rheumatoid factor, anti-CCP, and iron studies; and today&apos;s metabolic panel, pain, function, and grip scores. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Left thumb-base injection with triamcinolone 10 mg given today; no repeat sooner than three months, and no more than three or four a year. [S]</item><item>Continue diclofenac gel to the painful hand joints and acetaminophen up to 3 g a day; no regular oral NSAIDs; if a short oral course is ever needed, use the lowest dose for the shortest time with a creatinine and blood pressure check. [S]</item><item>Wear the hand-based thumb splint for carving and at night; start occupational hand therapy for thumb-stabilizing exercises, joint protection, and tool adaptation, such as padded handles and a lighter mallet. [S]</item><item>Reassess pain and function four to six weeks after the injection. [S]</item><item>No hydroxychloroquine, other disease-modifying drugs, or oral glucocorticoids, which do not help hand osteoarthritis; duloxetine only if pain stays high despite these measures. [S]</item><item>No routine repeat radiographs; consider thumb-base surgery only if pain and function fail to respond. [S]</item><item>Continue allopurinol 200 mg with six-monthly urate checks; no change because of the hand symptoms. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Castellano the same day. Mr. Buonarroti was clear that his goal is to keep carving; the plan is built around that, and he agreed to the splint and to resting his hand every 45 minutes. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care manages the plan and the post-injection review. Return to rheumatology if pain returns despite treatment, for a repeat injection, or for a surgical opinion. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
